              "Every English sentence is built from eight parts of speech, and a writer who cannot name them cannot diagnose their own errors. Nouns name people, places, things or ideas (engineer, laboratory, integrity). Pronouns stand in for nouns (he, she, it, they, which). Verbs express action or state of being (measure, is, becomes). Adjectives modify nouns (precise, blue, technical). Adverbs modify verbs, adjectives or other adverbs (quickly, very, almost). Prepositions relate nouns to other words (in, on, under, by). Conjunctions join words, phrases or clauses (and, but, because). Interjections express sudden emotion (oh, alas). Technical writing uses all eight, but the engineer's frequent errors cluster in verbs, prepositions and articles.",
