              "Articles and prepositions are the small words that betray an unpractised writer. English has three articles: 'a' and 'an' (indefinite, before consonant and vowel sounds respectively), and 'the' (definite, before a specific referent). Use 'a' before consonant sounds (a uniform, a one-off, a European - the leading sound is a consonant despite the vowel letter), 'an' before vowel sounds (an hour, an MBA, an honest answer - the leading sound is a vowel despite the consonant letter). Use 'the' when the noun is uniquely identified (the sun, the report you submitted yesterday, the first law of thermodynamics). Use no article with proper nouns (India, not 'the India'), with abstract nouns in general statements (patience is a virtue), and with plural count nouns in general statements (engineers solve problems).",
