              "A job application has two parts: a covering letter and a resume. The covering letter is a one-page formal letter that introduces the candidate, states the position applied for, summarises relevant qualifications and requests an interview. The resume is a structured one- or two-page document that lists personal details, education, technical skills, projects, experience (if any) and references. The covering letter follows the formal letter layout from section 3.3; the resume follows its own structure with headings, bullet lists and dates. Both must be tailored to the position - a generic application sent to fifty employers is visible from the first sentence and rejected at the first sort.",
              "Resume structure. Header: name, contact (phone, email, city). Career objective: one sentence stating the target role ('Seeking a graduate engineer position in automotive design'). Education: degree, branch, institution, year, percentage or CGPA, in reverse chronological order (most recent first). Technical skills: programming languages, software tools, lab instruments, certificates. Projects: title, role, duration, one-line description of what was done and the result. Experience (if any): company, role, duration, two or three bullet achievements. Achievements: academic, sports, cultural. Personal details: date of birth, languages known, address. References: one or two with name, designation, contact. Two pages is the upper limit for a fresher.",
