            number: "4.5",
            title: "Proposals: solicited vs unsolicited, structure",
            body: [
              "A proposal is a document that offers to do something - solve a problem, supply goods, perform research - for a defined price and within a defined timeframe. Proposals split into solicited and unsolicited. A solicited proposal responds to a Request for Proposal (RFP) issued by a client; the structure is dictated by the RFP, and the proposal must answer every requirement point by point or face disqualification. An unsolicited proposal is sent without an RFP, proposing an idea the client has not requested; it must work harder to establish the need before offering the solution. Solicited proposals have a higher success rate because the client has already committed to buying; unsolicited proposals succeed only when the idea is compelling and the timing is right.",
              "Proposal structure (formal). Front matter: cover, title page, letter of transmittal, table of contents, executive summary. Body: 1. Introduction (background and problem statement); 2. Need or justification (why the work matters); 3. Objectives (specific, measurable); 4. Methodology or plan of work (how the objectives will be met, with a work breakdown structure); 5. Timeline or schedule (Gantt chart with milestones); 6. Budget (manpower, materials, overhead, contingency); 7. Qualifications or team (who will do the work, past experience); 8. References (past clients, similar projects). End matter: appendices (team resumes, detailed cost sheets, technical specifications). The structure follows a fixed logic: problem -> need -> objectives -> method -> schedule -> budget -> qualifications -> evidence.",
              "Worked proposal skeleton. Client: Acme Industries, Indore. Title: 'Proposal for setting up a CNC machining cell at Acme Indore'. Executive summary: 'This proposal offers to design, install and commission a CNC machining cell at Acme Indore in 16 weeks for Rs 1.2 crore, with expected payback in 2.5 years.' Body: 1. Introduction (Acme's manual machining is at capacity and quality varies); 2. Need (CNC cell will triple throughput, halve rejection, enable complex parts); 3. Objectives (install 2 CNC mills and 1 CNC lathe, train 6 operators, achieve 95 percent first-pass yield within 8 weeks of commissioning); 4. Methodology (site survey, machine selection, civil and electrical prep, installation, training, commissioning); 5. Schedule (16-week Gantt); 6. Budget (machines Rs 90 lakh, civil Rs 12 lakh, training Rs 8 lakh, contingency Rs 10 lakh); 7. Qualifications (the bidder has installed 12 CNC cells in MP); 8. References (3 past clients).",
              "Persuasion principles in proposals. Establish the need before offering the solution - a proposal that jumps to the solution without justifying the need reads as self-serving. Quantify benefits in the client's units (payback years, rejection percent, throughput) - vague benefits ('improved efficiency') do not persuade. Address risks explicitly (delays, cost overruns, training gaps) with mitigations - hiding risks loses trust. Match the client's RFP language and section numbering exactly in a solicited proposal. Cite past performance with numbers ('installed 12 CNC cells, average commissioning 14 weeks'). Price competitively but not destructively - a price too low suggests the bidder has not understood the scope. The proposal is, ultimately, a promise to deliver; credibility is its currency.",
            ],
