              "Four specialised technical documents extend the report family. The project report documents a final-year project from problem to result; it follows the formal report structure but emphasises the literature review, methodology and results chapters. A technical description explains what a device or system is - its parts, their arrangement, their function - in enough detail for a reader to understand it but not necessarily build it. A process description explains how something is done or how it happens - the sequence of steps, their inputs and outputs - chronologically and causally. A user manual tells an end user how to operate a device safely and effectively; it is structured by task, not by component.",
              "Technical description structure. Definition: a one-sentence overall definition ('A centrifugal pump is a rotodynamic machine that converts rotational energy into fluid pressure'). Overall description: a paragraph giving the size, weight, major components and operating principle. Parts description: each major part in a separate paragraph, naming the part, its location, its appearance and its function ('The impeller, mounted on the shaft inside the casing, is a disc with curved vanes that accelerates the fluid'). Materials and finishes: what each part is made of and why. The order is whole to parts to materials, often following a spatial principle (top to bottom, outside in). Diagrams accompany each part; the reader should be able to point to each named component on a labelled drawing.",
              "Process description structure. Introduction: what the process is, who performs it, when and why. Equipment and materials: what is needed. Steps: a numbered sequence, each step stating who does what, with what, and what the result is ('Step 3: The operator heats the specimen to 850 deg C in the muffle furnace over 30 minutes; the result is a fully austenitised specimen'). Transitions: 'first', 'then', 'next', 'finally' signal order; 'because', 'as a result', 'consequently' signal cause. Conclusion: the state of the workpiece at the end. Process descriptions use the present tense for general processes ('the operator heats'), the past tense for a specific instance ('the operator heated'). The reader should be able to perform the process after one reading.",
              "User manual elements. Front matter: title, version, date, safety warnings (prominent), table of contents. Description of the product: what it is, what it does, major parts labelled in a diagram. Installation: prerequisites, tools, step-by-step procedure, post-installation checks. Operation: how to start, run, stop, and what to monitor. Maintenance: routine (daily, weekly, monthly) and preventive schedule. Troubleshooting: the symptom-cause-action table from section 2.4. Specifications: technical data, dimensions, weight, power. Appendices: spare parts list, warranty terms, contact information. The manual is task-structured: the reader looks up 'how do I start the motor?' and finds a numbered procedure, not a paragraph on motor theory. Safety warnings appear at the start and at each hazardous step.",
